        "math_academy_id": "q-131170",
        "problem": "Given that $F=\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,i+\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,j,$ what is $\\text{div}\\,F?$",
        "worked_solution": "If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ is a vector field on ${R}^{2}$ and the partial derivatives of the components of $F$ all exist, then the divergence of $F$ is the scalar field given by\n\n$$\n\\text{div}\\,F=\u2207\\cdot F=\\frac{\u2202P}{\u2202x}+\\frac{\u2202Q}{\u2202y}.\n$$\n\n  \n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\u2207\\cdot F & =\\frac{\u2202P}{\u2202x}+\\frac{\u2202Q}{\u2202y} \\\\  & =\\frac{\u2202}{\u2202x}(\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}})+\\frac{\u2202}{\u2202y}(\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{2xy}{{({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})}^{2}}-\\frac{2xy}{{({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})}^{2}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{4xy}{{({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
        "actual_result": "Correct",
